from common import ROOT
import json,shutil,hashlib
R=ROOT/'revision/review_v6';V5=ROOT/'revision/review_v5';text=(V5/'manuscript.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
abstract='''Climate predictors may improve rainfall calibration, but their value depends on where they enter a coherent forecast and which baseline is used. We compare weekly, aggregate and all-node climate adjustments for subseasonal rainfall in eight Australian domains using nested ECMWF validation, ACCESS-S2 and a previously unused 2020 forecast period. In retrospective ECMWF comparisons, H1, which adjusts aggregate forecasts before reconciliation, changes 28-day continuous ranked probability score by −5.82% relative to raw forecasts, −0.78% relative to rainfall-only weekly summation, −1.05% relative to climate-informed weekly summation and −0.33% relative to rainfall-only reconciliation, expressed as percentages of raw score. Their 95% intervals are respectively [−7.56, −4.14], [−1.42, −0.26], [−1.50, −0.61] and [−0.74, 0.06]%; the first three comparisons pass Holm adjustment. Weekly-only climate adjustment with reconciliation performs similarly to H1, whereas adjusting every node worsens scores. ACCESS-S2 also favours H1 over climate-informed summation, but that comparator underperforms rainfall-only summation in seven domains. In 2020, H1 improves on rainfall-only summation in three domains, and its predeclared comparison with climate-informed summation remains inconclusive. Northern circulation composites describe the wet-season setting without identifying a reliable gain predictor. The results motivate testing whether retaining rainfall-only components limits climate-adjustment noise; they do not establish that mechanism or a stable advantage over simple alternatives.'''
start=text.index('## Abstract\n\n')+len('## Abstract\n\n');end=text.index('\n\nKeywords:',start);text=text[:start]+abstract+text[end:]
def para(prefix,new):
 global text
 blocks=text.split('\n\n');ix=next(i for i,b in enumerate(blocks) if b.startswith(prefix));blocks[ix]=new;text='\n\n'.join(blocks)
para('The unresolved issue is how much a forecast gains', 'The unresolved issue is whether climate information and direct aggregate forecasts contribute beyond strong coherent alternatives. Recent subseasonal postprocessing improves ensemble probabilities (Guan et al. 2026), but does not establish where additional predictors should enter an accumulation hierarchy. We compare climate information at weekly, aggregate and all nodes, retaining raw forecasts, rainfall-only weekly sums and rainfall-only reconciliation. This separates improvement over a flexible comparator from improvement over simpler forecasts.')
para('The four additional ECMWF domains extend', 'Across eight ECMWF domains, H1 changes mean 28-day CRPS by −5.824% of raw score relative to raw forecasts (95% interval −7.564 to −4.140%; Holm p = 0.00004), −0.781% relative to BU-rain (−1.418 to −0.258%; p = 0.01462), −1.046% relative to BU-clim (−1.501 to −0.606%; p = 0.00084), and −0.331% relative to H0 (−0.742 to 0.056%; p = 0.13023). Favourable domains number eight, six, eight and five, respectively. Table 6 reports the complete exploratory four-comparison family; Fig. 5 and Supplementary Table S23 retain domain detail.')
para('The completed placement comparison changes the attribution.', 'Hweekly minus H1 is +0.025% of raw CRPS (95% interval −0.212 to 0.260%). Both improve on BU-clim by about 1%, whereas H2 minus BU-clim spans zero; H2 is worse than H1 in every domain (Supplementary Table S22). The post hoc score interaction, defined as (H2 minus Hweekly) minus (H1 minus H0), is +1.284% (0.954–1.646%; Supplementary Table S27). This measures how the aggregate adjustment changes when weeks are also adjusted. It combines marginal and covariance changes, since each configuration estimates its own W.')
para('Across eight ACCESS-S2 domains, H1 minus BU-clim', 'Across ACCESS-S2 domains, H1 minus BU-clim is −5.726% of raw CRPS (95% interval −7.659 to −3.900%; Holm p = 0.00009). However, BU-clim is worse than BU-rain in seven domains and worse than raw forecasts in NE, NW and EC. In NE, BU-rain, H1 and BU-clim score 17.806, 17.997 and 19.775 mm. H1 therefore beats a weak comparator here without beating the simpler weekly sum. H1 minus H0 is +0.468% (−0.556 to 1.451%; p = 0.440), leaving the incremental climate effect uncertain (Table 6; Supplementary Table S24).')
para('In the previously unused issued-forecast period, H1', 'In 2020, H1 beats BU-clim in six domains but BU-rain in only three: N, NW and EC. The predeclared H1-minus-BU-clim pooled difference is −0.923% (95% four-origin block interval −2.555 to 0.413%); longer blocks also include zero. The confirmation criterion is unmet, leaving insufficient information to establish transfer rather than demonstrating no effect (Table 6; Fig. 8). A post hoc normal approximation gives an 80%-power effect size of about 2.15% of training raw CRPS, conditional on this year’s block variability. This cannot quantify interannual power. Supplementary Note 7 reproduces the original locally dated protocol and explains the precision calculation.')
text=text.replace('### 4.12 Monsoon circulation explains the seasonal setting','### 4.12 Monsoon circulation describes the seasonal setting')
para('The main distinction is between improving a coherent forecast', 'The main distinction is between beating one calibrated comparator and demonstrating robust added value. ECMWF H1 improves on raw forecasts, BU-rain and BU-clim in the pooled retrospective comparison, but its difference from H0 remains uncertain. ACCESS-S2 favours H1 over BU-clim partly in a setting where weekly climate adjustment degrades the baseline. In 2020, H1 beats BU-rain in only three domains. These comparisons place the apparent reconciliation gain in its wider forecast context (Table 7).')
para('Rainfall-only marginals may constrain uncertain climate adjustments.', 'A testable explanation is that rainfall-only nodes constrain noise introduced by climate adjustment. Hweekly combines adjusted weeks with unadjusted aggregates; H1 combines unadjusted weeks with adjusted aggregates. Their similar scores, and the poorer H2 result, fit this explanation. They do not distinguish estimation noise from altered dispersion, dependence or covariance weights. Fixed-W ablations and stronger shrinkage would test these alternatives. The positive score interaction describes diminishing benefit from adjusting both layers, not information-theoretic redundancy or a demonstrated physical mechanism.')
para('Direct aggregate forecasts add value relative to climate-informed weekly summation', 'In eight-domain ECMWF cross-validation, H1 improves on raw forecasts and both weekly-summation baselines, but not clearly on rainfall-only reconciliation. Hweekly performs similarly, whereas all-node climate adjustment worsens the comparison. ACCESS-S2 also favours H1 over BU-clim, whose weakness against BU-rain limits that result as evidence for added aggregate information.')
para('Temporal validation qualifies this retrospective advantage.', 'Temporal validation changes the comparison. Northern 2015–2019 forward scores favour BU-clim over H1, and raw forecasts over both. The 2020 test is inconclusive, with H1 beating BU-rain in only three domains. One year supplies neither confirmation of stable transfer nor evidence that the effect is absent.')
para('Independent circulation data explain the wet-season setting', 'Circulation data describe a humid summer monsoon background but do not identify a reliable predictor of reconciliation gain. Retaining rainfall-only components may limit unstable climate adjustments; this remains a hypothesis requiring controlled covariance and regularisation tests. Forecast services should assess climate information against raw and simple coherent alternatives, with explicit attention to sample size and evaluation period.')
text=text.replace('Joint energy and variogram scores are reported in Supplementary Table S4.','Joint energy and variogram scores are reported in Supplementary Table S4 (Scheuerer and Hamill 2015b).')
(R/'manuscript.md').write_text(text,encoding='utf-8')
plan=V5/'operational_test_plan_20261025.md';shutil.copy2(plan,R/plan.name)
